From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:37:22 -0400
Subject: feat: screenshot codec off the UI thread, failures said (S-156)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Screenshot C9: the DevTools reply's JSON parse and base64 decode, the
PNG encode of a visible or region capture, the PNG decode of a full page
for the clipboard and the file write now run as one task per capture on
the screenshot writer sequence (new screenshot_codec.{h,cc}). Only the
clipboard write and the toast come back to the UI thread, in the order
the pictures arrived. The full-page client stops its 15-second timer
once the reply is here, so a reply queued behind another capture's
encode no longer times out.

Screenshot C10: a capture that does not end in a saved file says so
("Couldn't take the screenshot", "Copied · Couldn't save to
Downloads"); a full page whose PNG does not decode is still saved
("Saved to Downloads · Couldn't copy"). The failure toast
(ToastId::kSteddingCaptureFailed) has a close button, so it shows at the
actionable-only alert level. A closed private window's picture is no
longer copied after it closed.

Screenshot C11: the toast's "Show in Finder" reveals the path carried in
its own toast data instead of one process-wide last path.
Screenshot C12: little windows and peeks have no toasts; their captures
are saved and copied. C1, C3, C5 and C8 corrected to match.

Tests: ScreenshotCodecTest.*, ScreenshotToastTest.*, and
ScreenshotCaptureTest.TheCodecRunsOffTheUIThreadInOrder,
AFailedCaptureSaysSo, EachToastRevealsItsOwnFile,
ACaptureInALittleWindowIsSavedWithoutAToast. The fixture now drains
the writer before the test clipboard goes.

Why: plan WIN-14; a tall full page paused the window during our own
codec work, every failure ended silently, and two windows' toasts
revealed the same file.
Removable when: never; the remaining WIN-14 work (area cap, the
clipboard's own UI-thread encode, the 100 ms test) stays in BACKLOG
S-156.
